From: Silverletter maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: strip Broadcom vendor bits from legacy advertising reports

Based on Jose Miguel Ochoa's Apple/Broadcom fix:
https://github.com/t2linux/linux-t2-patches/blob/main/9002-Bluetooth-hci_event-strip-Broadcom-vendor-bits-from-adv-type.patch

MacBook9,1's UART BCM4350C0 also reports directed advertisements as
0x11/0x21/0xf1. Linux rejects these and never attempts to reconnect a
bonded LE mouse. Mask the vendor bits for Broadcom controllers only;
valid types remain unchanged and invalid low nibbles are still rejected.
Verified on MacBook9,1 with a Logitech MX Anywhere 3 for Mac.

--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
@@ -6418,5 +6418,9 @@
 		hci_store_wake_reason(hdev, &info->bdaddr, info->bdaddr_type);
 
+		/* Broadcom ROM firmware tags legacy reports in reserved bits. */
+		if (hdev->manufacturer == 15 && info->type > LE_ADV_SCAN_RSP)
+			info->type &= 0x0f;
+
 		if (info->length <= max_adv_len(hdev)) {
 			rssi = info->data[info->length];
 			process_adv_report(hdev, info->type, &info->bdaddr,
